The question of providing the benefit to employees by a company is to be answered keeping in view the commercial expediency of a company and such benefit is only a substitute for production incentive/bonus/welfare expenditure which are otherwise incurred by a company in the normal course of carrying on its business and are allowed as business deduction by the revenue without any whisper.

The expenditure agreed to be incurred by a company does not always mean that an amount should have gone out from the pocket of an assessee. It has been so held by the Supreme Court in various cases and the same has been repeated in the case of Madras Industrial Investment Corpn. Ltd. (supra). It would include a case where an assessee incurs loss by failing or refraining to get it in the normal course. In simple terms it means that an assessee need not necessarily spend any amount in cash from out of its pocket as it may at times forego its opportunity to receive cash instead of availing a benefit to others or incurring a liability on its own. This may be understood as "cost of opportunity or loss of opportunity" to the extent of value that is foregone by an assessee. Such a principle was recognized long ago by the House of Lords in Tamplin & Sons Brewery (Brighter) Ltd. v. Nash [1951] 32 TC 415. In this case, certain premises were taken on lease by the assessee for the purpose of sub-letting the same to persons who would be vending the assessee’s products and sub-leases were granted at concessional rents in order to induce the sub-lessee to market the assessee’s products. The loss sustained by the assessee on the rentals, being the difference between the rent paid by it and the sub-rents realised was held to be permissible deduction.

Conclusion

9. Though there is clear distinction between an expenditure and a loss and section 37 is a residuary section allowing items of expenditure and not losses, the distinction is sometimes not quite clear and the loss is allowed as an expenditure. In the issue on hand, it appears as though a company suffers a loss being the differential price of share of Rs. X (price of share in the market minus the price at which a company offers a share to its employees), but in reality this amount is passed on to an employee in the form of ESOS/ESOP which otherwise in the company’s coffers shall have resulted in surplus to that extent. In that sense, the benefit provided by a company towards ESOS/ESOP is to be treated as expenditure in a commercial sense and the same can be claimed as it is incidental to the business of the company.

The benefit provided by the company is an allowable deduction for another reason that in order to claim deduction, it is enough to show that the money (differential sale price in monetary terms) is expended not out of necessity and with a view to direct and immediate benefit to the trade but voluntarily and on the grounds of commercial expediency and in order indirectly to facilitate the carrying on of the business. This principle enunciated by Viscount Cave, Lord Chancellor in Artherton v. British Insulated & Helsby Cables Ltd. [1925] 10 TC 155 (HL) has indeed become classic and is viewed by Courts as almost a statutory provision on the subject of the deductibility of the expenses. Applying this principle, many Courts, including the Supreme Court, have been holding that pension or gratuity or bonus paid to employees, though unenforceable as being time barred, was allowable deductions. It was so held in the following cases on—

(i) Eastern Investments Ltd. v. CIT [1951] 20 ITR 1 (SC);

(ii) Binodiram Balchand v. CIT [1963] 48 ITR 548 (MP);

(iii) Calcutta Landing & Shipping Co. Ltd. v. CIT [1967] 65 ITR 1 (Cal.);

